In May and June of 2025, before rstd had a BLAS and before SRAPNOL had a name,
I had a run of long conversations with ChatGPT about linear algebra libraries,
SNOPT and sparse matrices. Now that the BLAS port is close to feature complete,
and SRAPNOL has a basic design, I reread the export, this time with Claude
helping fact-check and summarize, to see which of those ideas made it into code,
which should not have, and which are still worth considering today (if any).

# Where it started

Most of the scientific code I work with regularly lived (and still lives) in
Fortran: matrix products, eigendecompositions, inverses, trigonometry and
elliptic integrals. I wanted a language with more community behind it than
Modern Fortran--albeit scrappy, robust, and prolific as the language and its
community still is in our field.  While Python is also very common in the field
as well, leveraging Numpy, SciPy, and/or Pandas to do the vectorized operations
that allow things to still run at scale while being "better supported" by the
community... I also wanted a focused, strongly-typed, data-oriented code/design
style.  In terms of options with better modern community adoption than Fortran,
Zig won me over for a variety of reasons, beating out C and Rust.
Zig is procedural like Fortran, but with portable SIMD through `@Vector` instead
of `immintrin.h` and `#ifdef`s.

In hindsight today, Odin is also an interesting option still worthy of
consideration for future endeavors, but for now, I put my money on Zig.
The main thing that drew my appeal after beginning to work with it
was its self-contained build system and goals of independence from external
tooling and dependencies, even going so far as to try to replace LLVM, the engine
behind just about every non-GCC compiler today.  A very bold move, but one that
seems to have captured the attention of very important players in the CS world.
Odin may have reached a v1.0 first, but it's a batteries-included language that
still depends on classic build systems.  By contrast, Zig's more minimalistic
approach gives you only what you need, but gives you ultimate control over every
aspect to almost an assembly-level granularity, while still relying on
the ecosystem and explicit dependencies for additional functionality.
While Zig's leadership may suffer from classic ADHD scope creep (hey, it takes
one to know one) and tends to try to absorb more capabilities, it tends to try
to shed core functionalities to spinoff external libraries at the same time.

The churn of Zig's development and its evolving ecosystem can be seen as a risk,
along with their fierce opposition to AI usage in the core community, which I
think is what led to the notorious conversion of Bun from Zig to Rust earlier
this year.  Ironically enough, while that story was unfolding, I was in the
midst of taking some Go applications I was growing frustrated with trying to
debug, and worked with Claude to convert them to Zig with wild success.
I called it the Reverse Bun.  Meanwhile, Ghostty and TigerBeetle continue to be
cornerstones of the Zig ecosystem, providing more confidence that the language
will continue to be viable and supported in the long term, as well as serve as
voices of reason within the community when breaking changes loom too large.
Zig is here to stay, and it has quickly become the foundation of everything I
work on these days wherever possible.

The first conclusion from the 2025 chats with ChatGPT held up: Fortran is fast
because of what its semantics let the compiler assume (arguments do not alias),
not because of instructions C cannot reach.
Fortran does a good job of abstracting away the low-level details of memory layout
and loop optimizations, letting the compiler focus on generating efficient code,
but it does so at the expense of fine-grained control or, critical for developers
truly chasing every last ounce of performance, insight into why or how any of it
works short of a disassembler.

# The BLAS plan that did not survive

The 2025 plan was bindings: MKL on Intel, Accelerate on Apple silicon, BLIS everywhere else,
picked at compile time and statically linked, one executable per backend. At one point, the plan
was a launcher that spawns the right static executable for the machine.

None of that exists in our implementation today, though I suppose it still could.
Rstd's BLAS is a native Zig port of OpenBLAS's structure, and selecting a
CPU core fills a table of kernels once at startup.  The worry about run-time
dispatch that drove the 2025 design turned out to be a non-issue.

For a MacOS target, Accelerate did survive from the original proposal as the default
run-time route in lieu of the custom OpenBLAS or other open-source kernels.
Apple Silicon has many specialized instructions that Accelerate can leverage
efficiently but that are not publicly documented.  Lacking any commonly accepted
white-room implementations or reverse engineering of this extended ISA,
Accelerate remains the practical choice to maximize performance on Apple Silicon.

We do still draw inspiration for our threading model and some of the kernels we
have since tweaked from BLIS.  But there are many influences on our linear algebra
implementation beyond BLIS, including ideas from LAPACK, ReLAPACK, Faer,
libflame/AOCL-libFRAME, LINPACK, EISPACK, 1988 open-source MATLAB,
and our own experiments with Zig's capabilities.
Many of these conceptual, but often some code is borrowed or adapted as well.
Ideas also drew inspiration from benchmarks against or clean-room observations
of the likes of Eigen, MKL, or even GLSL and SPIR-V spec for understanding how GPUs handle
linear algebra computations to help genericize concepts.

# Why not just use SNOPT

SNOPT's proprietary license rules it out for usage by open source libraries,
and it has always seemed better suited than IPOPT to things of interest to me
like spacecraft trajectories. Those problems have a few tight active constraints,
often start infeasible, and get solved in chains where each run starts from the last.
An active-set SQP method identifies the active set exactly and is well-suited to warm-starts;
an interior-point method approaches the bounds from inside and restarts poorly.
This desire for an SNOPT alternative led to the creation of SRAPNOL:
an SQP solver in Zig, written from Gill, Murray and Saunders's papers, similar
to SNOPT's `snOptA` interface.

# The sparse rabbit hole

The longest conversation was about storing SNOPT's sparse Jacobians.
I proposed my own format: one array of values, one array of linear indices
(`row + col * nrows`) sorted column-major, and a small table pointing into them
every 128 columns instead of a pointer per column. ChatGPT named it
"BLCOO, bucketed linear COO", and helped me design kernels for it over a long thread.

Some of it was right. Computing the pattern of a sparse product before its values, and reusing
that pattern while the inputs keep theirs, is how real libraries do it. Gustavson's
column-by-column product is the right algorithm for column-major storage.

Rereading, though, a lot of it was just ChatGPT agreeing with me:

- I asked whether transposing `x`, multiplying, and transposing back gives `A x`. It said yes.
  It gives `Aᵀ x`. I switched my default layout back to column-major on that answer; the choice
  happened to be right, for a different reason.
- After proposing Gustavson's method, which only reads columns of `A`, it told me a sparse
  product needs rows of `A`, and the thread ended on transposed views that were never needed.
- It called BLCOO innovative (the audacity!).
  The problem it solves--more columns than nonzeros--already has a name: hypersparse.
  It also has a standard format: DCSC, from 2008.  SuiteSparse:GraphBLAS ships
  with it. Meanwhile, BLCOO saves only the column pointers, about 3% in ChatGPT's
  own example, and a 32-bit linear index stops working past a 65536-by-65536 matrix.

In a later thread, ChatGPT forgot what it named the storage format already, and
it read BLCOO as "block COO" and suggested it for video streaming.  Oh, the old
hallucinogenic days of yore.

Currently, SRAPNOL stores its Jacobian the way SNOPT does, in compressed sparse columns.
Pricing, the step that picks which constraint to free next, takes one dot product per column,
exactly what that layout is good at.  I still want to investigate alternative
formats like actual DCSC or my BLCOO variant.

# What I took from it

The chats were good at vocabulary and at mapping the territory: the Sparse BLAS, SuiteSparse,
GraphBLAS, what "kernel" means, which test sets SNOPT is measured on. They were bad at telling
me no. When the answer depended on my own idea being right, it usually was, according to
ChatGPT.

I have since learned to ask for prior art by name before believing something is
new, and I ask it to restate the plan every few messages because sometimes it
can get lost in the context. Especially since I had to correct its picture of
BLCOO three times in one thread.  But Claude is not immune to this either, and
I have learned to ask it to write down its to-do list in text files rather than
trust the to-do list it shows in chat will remain up to date or have items
mysteriously fall off of that list.

The ideas that survived review are recorded in rstd's linear algebra design notes,
as a planned sparse matrix section and a pair of to-do items, and in
SRAPNOL design notes.  The next sparse work is a real LU factorization rather
than just focusing on sparse matrix products.
